      "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}2f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨1,\\,-3,\\,2⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-⟨1,\\,-3,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,3,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the scalar multiplication rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,3,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,6,\\,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
